  "business-rules.md": "# Business rules\n\n<!-- BA. Rules, thresholds, tolerances, rounding, and edge cases already decided; one rule per bullet.\nWhen a story depends on a rule, the ticket cites it instead of restating it. At handover, epic requirements are folded in here.\nWhen a rule was chosen over an alternative, a `Rejected: <option> — <reason>` line goes directly beneath it, so the next reader finds the argument instead of re-proposing the option that lost. -->\n\n- Sample rule: amounts are rounded to two decimal places, half away from zero\n- Sample rule: a request older than 30 days is treated as expired\n",
  "epic-lane.md": "---\nid: \"TICKET-ID\"                            # equals the file name: tickets/<id>.md\ntitle: \"Short title in business language\"\ntype: epic                                 # an epic groups stories; its stories carry the acceptance criteria\nstatus: draft                              # draft | open | archived | dropped. Document lifecycle only; work status stays in the tracker\nlane: L                                    # L: an epic | P: an epic of a whole project\n# Tickets that must reach done before this one can start.\n# depends_on: [\"OTHER-ID\"]\n# Ids in the tracker, a map keyed by adapter name; values are strings.\n# tracker: { github-issues: \"1234\" }\nui: false\n# Optional design reference URL for the epic's stories.\n# design: \"https://example.com/design/...\"\n# Written by `accord approve` after the owner says yes; never edit by hand.\n# approved_hash:\n---\n\n## Intent\n<!-- At most five lines: why, for whom, how success is measured, and what this does not do. -->\n\n## Vetoes\n<!-- What the owner forbids, in the owner's own words, one item per line. They bind every story of the epic. -->\n\n## Decisions\n<!-- Settled answers, one per line: - D-01 <decision> — \"<the owner's sentence>\" -->\n\n## Corrections\n<!-- Every correction the owner makes, verbatim, appended and never edited. -->\n\n## Open questions\n<!-- `- [ ]` items; Ready stays blocked while any is unchecked. -->\n\n## Approach\n<!-- The chosen option and why, rejected options and why, and the split into stories with their depends_on order. -->\n",
  "epic.md": "---\nid: \"TICKET-ID\"                            # equals the file name: tickets/<id>.md\ntitle: \"Short title in business language\"\ntype: epic                                 # an epic groups stories and bugs, holds intent and requirements for its children, is linted but never gated\nstatus: draft                              # draft | open | archived | dropped. Document lifecycle only; work status stays in the tracker\n# Leave unset on an epic; a story or bug uses it to name its epic. Grouping is by parent; there is no per-epic folder.\n# parent: \"EPIC-ID\"\n# Ids in the tracker, a map keyed by adapter name; values are strings.\n# tracker: { github-issues: \"1234\" }\n# When true, Ready requires a design reference; the Intent note below says which one this profile expects.\nui: false\n# Optional design reference URL. Allowed on epics too.\n# design: \"https://example.com/design/...\"\n# Unconfirmed assumptions block Ready.\n# assumptions:\n#   - { text: \"...\", confirmed: false }\n---\n\n## Intent\n<!-- BA. Why, for whom, how success is measured, and explicit non-goals. About five lines.\nDescribe observable behaviour in business language; never name tables, endpoints, libraries, or screens.\nTerms go in product/glossary.md; rules already decided go in product/business-rules.md.\nAn epic is never gated; design: may hold the epic-level design link for its child stories. -->\n\n## Requirements\n<!-- BA. One EARS line per requirement: WHEN <trigger> the system SHALL <response>, and its siblings. 5 to 15 lines.\nOrdering of child stories, when needed, is prose here in v0.1 (ticket dependencies are deferred). -->\n\n## Open questions\n<!-- BA. `- [ ]` items; Ready stays blocked while any is unchecked. -->\n",
